=begin pod

=TITLE History: Raku Compilers

=SUBTITLE Raku compilers, both current and historical

Raku is a language specification and, just like C or C++, it
encourages multiple compilers for the language.

=head1 Current Compilers

=head2 L<Rakudo™|https://rakudo.org/> and NQP

Rakudo runs on L<MoarVM|http://moarvm.org/> and the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).
It's developed on L<GitHub|http://github.com/rakudo/rakudo>.

NQP is B<not> a Raku compiler. NQP is a small subset of Raku used as a 
foundational tool to help write compilers (including 
L<Rakudo|https://github.com/rakudo/rakudo/blob/master/src/main.nqp>)
and libraries. Development happens on L<GitHub|https://github.com/raku/nqp/>.

=head1 Historical Compilers

=head2 L<Niecza|http://github.com/sorear/niecza>

Niecza is a compiler targeting the Common Language Runtime (.NET and Mono).  Its 
goal is to explore the static aspects of Raku and the possibilities for 
compile-time optimization.

=head2 STD and viv

=head3 L<STD|/compilers/std-viv>

STD is Larry Wall's reference implementation of the Raku grammar, parser 
generator, and some of the semantic verifier. STD is an abbreviation of 
"Standard". It is written in Raku, transliterated into Perl 5 by viv.  It is 
also used by various phases of several other compilers (mildew, perlito, elf).

The Raku standard grammar is written in Raku, and specifies how a Raku program 
is parsed.

The source code can be found on 
L<github in the perl6/std project|http://github.com/perl6/std/>
in the file L<STD.pm6|https://github.com/perl6/std/blob/master/STD.pm6>.

Both STD.pm and viv are included in the 
L<STD|https://metacpan.org/release/STD> package on CPAN.

=head3 L<viv|/compilers/std-viv>

viv ("vi" to "v", 6 to 5) is a compiler that uses STD as its front-end. STD 
itself uses viv to build. It is developed along with STD.

B<viv> should be read as B<VI ➔ V>, where V and I are Roman
numerals. So it's a translator from Perl 6 to Perl 5, expressly designed
to run STD.pm6 - it compiles to Perl 5 code only features that are needed
to run STD.pm6. However it can be used by other compilers as a base to
produce code for other backends.

=head2 L<Perlito|http://fglock.github.io/Perlito/>

Perlito, also known as MiniPerl6, is a subset of Raku, designed as a light 
bootstrapping language.  You can 
L<try it online|http://fglock.github.io/Perlito/perlito/perlito6.html>.  It is
developed L<here|http://github.com/fglock/Perlito/>.

=head2 L<Pugs|https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pugs_(programming)>

Pugs is written in Haskell.  Maintenance releases for successive versions of ghc 
are L<published|http://hackage.haskell.org/package/Pugs> to Hackage.

=end pod
